// ============================================================================
// two-dice-sums.epher -- the exact distribution of the sum of two dice
//
// Two fair dice give 36 equally likely ordered pairs (a, b), so the sum
// s = a + b occurs in s - 1 of those pairs when s <= 7 and in 13 - s of
// them when s > 7 (the distribution is triangular, peaking at s = 7).
// The probabilities are the counts over 36, and every one of them is an
// exact fraction here: 6/36 = 1/6 for the sum 7.
//
// Source: the standard counting argument; Feller, "An Introduction to
// Probability Theory and Its Applications", volume 1, chapter 1.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/probability/games/two-dice-sums.epher"
// Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
// ============================================================================

// ---- results ----
// The count of pairs giving each sum from 2 to 12: {1, 2, ..., 6, ...}.
for s in 2 to 12 do if s <= 7 then s - 1 else 13 - s

// The same counts over 36, as exact probabilities.
for s in 2 to 12 do (if s <= 7 then s - 1 else 13 - s) / 36

// The anchor: P(sum 7) = 6/36 = 1/6, and the two extreme sums are the
// rarest with 1/36 each.
print("P(sum 7):", 6 / 36)
print("P(sum 2):", 1 / 36)
print("P(sum 12):", 1 / 36)

// Self-checks: the eleven counts add to the 36 ordered pairs, so the
// probabilities sum to 1, and the distribution is symmetric about 7.
print("the counts add to 36:", 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 == 36)
// ---- expected output ----
// = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1}
// = {1/36, 1/18, 1/12, 1/9, 5/36, 1/6, 5/36, 1/9, 1/12, 1/18, 1/36}
// = P(sum 7): 1/6
// = P(sum 2): 1/36
// = P(sum 12): 1/36
// = the counts add to 36: true
